Johnson's algorithm pseudocode:
- Work on SCCs (only non-trivial SCCs contain cycles, plus self-loops).
- For each SCC, run Johnson's circuit finding.

Johnson's circuit algorithm (from the paper):
```
blocked = set, B = map node->set
stack = []
circuits = []
function circuit(v, start, adjSub):
  found = false
  stack.push(v); blocked.add(v)
  for w in adjSub[v]:
    if w == start: circuits.push(stack copy + start); found = true
    elif w not in blocked:
      if circuit(w, start, adjSub): found = true
  if found: unblock(v) and cascade via B
  else: for w in adjSub[v]: B[w].add(v)
  stack.pop()
  return found
```
Main loop: for each start node s in SCC (ordered), consider subgraph induced by nodes >= s (in some ordering), find least SCC with... The classic version: iterate s, build subgraph of SCC containing only nodes >= s, find SCCs of that subgraph, pick the SCC containing the least vertex, run circuit from that vertex. To keep deterministic and simpler: iterate s over sorted nodes; subgraph = nodes >= s (sorted order index); compute SCCs of subgraph; if s is in a non-trivial SCC (or has self-loop), run circuit(s). That's a valid variant. Complexity fine for 1000/5000/20 cycles.

